<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint-Lizier</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lugdunum Consoranorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint-Lizier is a small town in Ariège in the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, the Roman Lugdunum Consoranorum, chief town of the Consoranni. It later took the name of its early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> Lycerius and was the seat of the bishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/couserans">Couserans</a> until 1801. Its Romanesque cathedral and cloister are part of the World Heritage routes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-lizier.jpg" alt="Saint-Lizier" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olybrius · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint-Lizier-_Clo%C3%AEtre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
